00:00:08.319 --> 00:00:09.360
HEATHER CROSS: Hi, there.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.182
Heather here.

00:00:10.182 --> 00:00:12.390
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And hello
again from me, Priyanka,

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.520
a customer engineer
here at Google.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.270
On today's episode, we'll be
discussing Deployment Manager.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.760
As our customers use
Cloud in bigger ways,

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.470
it's becoming
increasingly important

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.680
to simplify Cloud management.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.180
Deployment Manager is an
infrastructure deployment

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.550
service that
automates the creation

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.254
and management of Google
Cloud Platform resources.

00:00:32.254 --> 00:00:33.420
HEATHER CROSS: That's right.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.184
And we'll also run
through a quick demo

00:00:35.184 --> 00:00:36.600
of the self-paced
lab, where we'll

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.960
use Deployment Manager to create
a simple configuration file,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.915
use that file to
create a deployment,

00:00:41.915 --> 00:00:44.040
then view the deployment
information on a manifest.

00:00:49.817 --> 00:00:51.900
So let's walk through how
Deployment Manager helps

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.539
simplify Cloud management.

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:55.080
I like to think of
Deployment Manager

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.555
as a cookbook combining all
the recipes into one place.

00:00:57.555 --> 00:00:58.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.020
And think of each
resource as a recipe.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.740
I like the fact that
Deployment Manager

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.370
supports a repeatable
deployment process.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.970
By creating configuration files,
which define the resources,

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.370
the process can be
repeated over and over

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:12.371
with consistent results.

00:01:12.371 --> 00:01:14.536
HEATHER CROSS: And keep in
mind declarative language

00:01:14.536 --> 00:01:16.710
can be used in creating
those configuration files.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.110
I can say exactly what I
want the configuration to be,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.240
and I just let the system
figure out what steps to take.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.490
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Absolutely.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.360
Configurations can
get really complex.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.110
A basic configuration file might
be enough for simple workloads,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:29.940
but for more complex
architectures

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.520
and configurations
that you plan to reuse,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.130
you can break your
configuration into templates.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.950
Templates allow you to
separate your configuration out

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.590
into different pieces
that you can reuse

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:41.915
across different deployments.

00:01:41.915 --> 00:01:43.290
HEATHER CROSS:
Deployment Manager

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.150
allows you to easily
deploy systems

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.250
in a variety of
locations and provides

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.955
control needed to easily
roll out new services.

00:01:49.955 --> 00:01:51.330
It also gives you
the flexibility

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.005
to deploy several versions
of your code simultaneously.

00:01:54.005 --> 00:01:55.380
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
It also allows

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.350
you to change a deployment
by adding or removing

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.570
resources, and
updating properties

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.110
of existing resources.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:08.639
Qwiklabs time.

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.700
In this lab, you will use
the gcloud Command-Line tool

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.340
to create a simple
configuration file.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.880
Use that file to
create a deployment.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.842
Then view deployment
information in a manifest.

00:02:18.842 --> 00:02:20.550
HEATHER CROSS: You
can check out the link

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.270
to start the Qwiklab here.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:25.520
Keep in mind that this will take
about 30 minutes to complete.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.340
So far in the lab, you've
created a configuration.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.770
You specified some values
in the vm.yaml file,

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.400
such as your project ID
and the VM image to deploy.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.436
To deploy the configuration,
you run this gcloud command.

00:02:39.436 --> 00:02:41.310
You will see the status
of your configuration

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.410
while it's being deployed.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:47.627
Upon completion, you will see
the state change to complete.

00:02:47.627 --> 00:02:49.710
You can then view information
about the deployment

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.280
by running the describe command.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.152
Using the manifest ID
output in the last command,

00:02:55.152 --> 00:02:56.735
you can see your
deployments manifest.

